# JOS-251 — LINE ENDINGS FOR THE FILES THAT CANNOT TAKE A CRLF CHECKOUT.
#
# This machine runs `core.autocrlf=true`, so git checks a text file out with CRLF and normalises it
# back to LF on the way in. That works for hand-edited source. It does NOT work for a file a script
# rewrites: `scripts/scrape-spells.ts` writes exactly the bytes the MediaWiki API returned (LF) and
# `JSON.stringify` output (LF), so after every run the working tree holds LF where git expects
# CRLF — and `git status` reports 1,963 modified files forever while `git diff` correctly reports
# no change at all. That is the "stray noise in the tree" the integration rules forbid, and it is
# worst exactly where it matters most: the whole point of the revision-keyed cache is that a
# re-scrape produces a VISIBLY empty diff.
#
# `eol=lf` tells git to check these out the way the scraper writes them. The stored blobs do not
# change (they were already LF); only the checkout does.
scripts/sources/cache/** text eol=lf
src/main/data/spells.json text eol=lf

# .nvmrc joins them for a DIFFERENT reason. Nothing rewrites it; the problem is
# that nvm reads it as a bare version string, so a trailing carriage return
# becomes part of the version it looks for and matches nothing installed. The
# stored blob is already LF — this pin is about the CHECKOUT, which on a box
# running autocrlf=true would otherwise hand nvm a CR it cannot use.
.nvmrc text eol=lf

# EVERY SOURCE FILE PRETTIER FORMATS, for the SAME autocrlf mechanism as above —
# and this one is a gate, not just tree noise.
#
# prettier.config.mjs sets `endOfLine: 'lf'`, and Prettier reads the WORKING TREE,
# never the git blob. The stored blobs here are already LF, so nothing about what
# git records changes; what changes is that autocrlf=true smudges each of these to
# CRLF *on checkout*, and `npm run format:check` then reads CRLF where the config
# says LF and reds the file. Without these lines, every fresh checkout on a box
# running autocrlf=true — which is the Git-for-Windows default, and is what
# GitHub's `windows-latest` runners do — fails the format gate on essentially the
# whole tree.
#
# THIS IS NOT HYPOTHETICAL. It was the real failure on this branch's first CI run:
# `Format check` red on 1493 files. The gap survived the Prettier plan's own Task 5
# investigation because the two tests used to "disprove" it could not see it —
# `git hash-object` applies the same clean filter as the stored blob (so a CRLF
# working file and its LF blob hash identically), and `git checkout -- <file>` is a
# silent no-op on a file git's stat cache already thinks is unchanged, so the smudge
# filter never ran. The final whole-branch review caught it by reproducing an actual
# fresh checkout with `git checkout-index -a --prefix=<tmp>/` — which does NOT skip
# files — and matching the corruption count to CI's failure exactly.
#
# One line per extension on purpose: .gitattributes patterns are fnmatch, NOT shell
# globs — `*.{ts,tsx}` is a literal filename here, not brace-expansion. The list
# mirrors package.json's `format`/`format:check` glob exactly; if that glob ever
# gains an extension, it has to gain a line here in the same commit.
*.ts text eol=lf
*.tsx text eol=lf
*.mts text eol=lf
*.cts text eol=lf
*.js text eol=lf
*.jsx text eol=lf
*.mjs text eol=lf
*.cjs text eol=lf
